<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kosovo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kosovo is a landlocked region in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pristina">Pristina</a> and a large Albanian majority. It was a heartland of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and the scene of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kosovo">Battle of Kosovo</a> in 1389, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> of 1912–1913. After the war of 1998–1999 it came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> administration, and it declared independence from Serbia in 2008.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kosovo.jpg" alt="Kosovo" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cradel (current version), earlier version by Ningyou · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kosovo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kosovo covers 10,887 km2 in the centre of Southeast Europe. Most of its borders run along mountains, notably the Accursed Mountains in the west, a continuation of the Dinaric Alps, and the Šar Mountains in the southeast. Between them lie two major plains, the Kosovo Plain in the east and the Metohija Plain in the west. The name comes from Kosovo Polje, Blackbird Field, the karst field where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kosovo">Battle of Kosovo</a> was fought in 1389. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the western territory was called Metohija, because large portions of land belonged to Serbian Orthodox monasteries and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> call it Dukagjini.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest evidence of organised settlement belongs to the Neolithic Starčevo and Vinča cultures. In antiquity the area was the core of Dardania, named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dardanians">Dardani</a>, a Paleo-Balkan people usually described as Illyrian, though scholars debate whether they were Illyrian or Thracian. Dardania was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> in the 1st century BCE and became a separate Roman province in the 3rd century CE. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> the region faced Slavic migrations in the 6th and 7th centuries, and control passed between the Byzantines and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a>.</p><p>In the 13th century Kosovo became integral to the medieval Serbian state and the establishment of the Serbian Patriarchate. Ottoman expansion in the late 14th and 15th centuries brought the fall of the Serbian Empire, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kosovo">Battle of Kosovo</a> of 1389, in which a Serbian-led coalition fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, is regarded as one of its defining moments. Various dynasties, chiefly the Branković, governed afterwards. The Ottoman Empire fully conquered Kosovo after the Second Battle of Kosovo and ruled for nearly five centuries until 1912. The Ottoman Vilayet of Kosovo, created in 1877, was the first time the name was applied to a wider area.</p><p>Kosovo was a centre of the Albanian Renaissance and saw Albanian revolts in 1910 and 1912. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> of 1912–1913 it was ceded to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it became an Autonomous Province within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. Tensions between the Albanian and Serb communities culminated in the Kosovo War of 1998 and 1999, after which the Yugoslav Army withdrew and the United Nations Interim Administration Mission in Kosovo was established.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kosovo declared its independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> on 17 February 2008. Its recognition is partial, and Serbia does not officially recognise it as a state. The capital and largest city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pristina">Pristina</a>, and the euro is the official currency. Four Serbian Orthodox churches and monasteries, including Visoki Dečani, the Patriarchate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pec">Peja</a>, Our Lady of Ljeviš and Gračanica, are inscribed as the World Heritage Site Medieval Monuments in Kosovo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kosovo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Armenia ratified the Treaty on Conventional Armed Forces in Europe in 1992, acceded to the Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty in 1993, joined the Collective Security Treaty Organisation and sent troops to NATO's KFOR in <b>Kosovo</b> in 2004; Russia keeps a base at Gyumri as a deterrent against Turkey.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Most of this diversity lay in the autonomous provinces of <b>Kosovo</b> and Vojvodina, and the Albanian population was concentrated in Kosovo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 2016 about 700 troops were stationed abroad: 100 with the NATO-led ISAF force in Afghanistan, 210 in <b>Kosovo</b> under KFOR and 160 in Bosnia and Herzegovina.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hungary">Hungary · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the newly gained areas an estimated 49,000–130,000 Albanians were expelled, settling mainly in <b>Kosovo</b>, events that marked the beginning of the Serbian-Albanian conflict; from 1830 to 1876 up to 150,000 Albanians had already emigrated or been expelled from the principality.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom united some twelve million people in 1921, rising to about sixteen million by 1941, from regions with very different pasts: former Habsburg Slovenia, Croatia, Vojvodina and Bosnia-Herzegovina, and the formerly Ottoman lands of Serbia, Montenegro, <b>Kosovo</b> and Macedonia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Within its neutrality Austria has a tradition of UN-led peacekeeping and humanitarian missions, with larger contingents in Bosnia and <b>Kosovo</b> and the all-volunteer Austrian Forces Disaster Relief Unit, able to deploy in ten hours.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-austria">Republic of Austria · Military</a></li></ul></section>
